            <ArticleTitle>[Determination of plasma and sputum substance P content in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or asthma].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1314-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the role of substance P (SP) in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) or asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Plasma and sputum samples were obtained from 26 COPD patients and 20 asthmatic patients as well as 12 healthy subjects for measurement of SP content.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients with COPD had significantly higher levels of SP in the plasma (7.9+/-2.6 pmol/L) and sputum (53.8+/-12.5 pmol/L) than the healthy subjects (3.6+/-1.7 pmol/L and 6.2+/-2.3 pmol/L, respectively, P&lt;0.01). The asthmatic patients also had significantly higher SP levels (8.3+/-3.1 pmol/L and 46.9+/-10.2 pmol/L, respectively) than the healthy subjects, but there was no significant difference between COPD and asthmatic patients (P&gt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SP may be involved in the airway inflammation process in COPD and asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
